Insight 1: Department-wise Attrition
Priority area for investigation
Finding:

The Sales department has the highest attrition rate at 20.63%, followed by Human Resources at 19.05%.

Research & Development has the lowest rate at 13.84%.

Business interpretation:

Sales employees may be experiencing challenges related to targets, incentives, workload or career progression. These possibilities require further investigation.

Recommendation:

Management should review sales targets, incentive structures, workload distribution and career progression opportunities.

Insight 2: Age-wise Attrition
Finding:

Employees under 30 have the highest attrition rate at 27.91%.

Business interpretation:

Younger employees may have different expectations regarding career growth, compensation, work environment and professional development.

Recommendation:

Investigate early-career development programmes, mentoring, onboarding and promotion opportunities.

Insight 3: Income Band vs Attrition
Finding:
Income Band	Attrition Rate
Low	21.76%
Medium	11.14%
High	8.90%

Business interpretation:

The lower-income category has considerably higher attrition than the other categories.

Recommendation:

Review salary competitiveness, compensation structures, benefits and early-career salary progression.

Insight 4: Overtime vs Attrition
Strongest observed difference
Finding:
- Overtime employees: 30.53% attrition.
- Non-overtime employees: 10.44% attrition.
The difference is approximately 20.09 percentage points.

Business interpretation:

Employees working overtime have substantially higher attrition in this dataset.

Recommendation:

Investigate workload distribution, overtime frequency, staffing requirements, compensation and work-life balance.

Insight 5: Job Satisfaction vs Attrition
Finding:
- Low satisfaction: 19.68%.
- Medium satisfaction: 16.52%.
- High satisfaction: 11.33%.
Business interpretation:

Attrition is higher among employees reporting lower job satisfaction.

Recommendation:

Conduct employee engagement surveys and investigate management support, recognition, workplace environment and career development.

Insight 6: Tenure Group vs Attrition
Important retention opportunity
Finding:

Employees with 0–2 years of tenure have the highest attrition rate at 29.82%.

Employees with 11+ years of tenure have the lowest rate at 8.13%.

Business interpretation:

The first two years appear to be an important employee retention period.

Recommendation:

Introduce structured onboarding, mentorship, regular performance discussions and early-career development programmes.
